  "text": "Three quick ones to close. An RDD is a low-level collection of objects Spark can't see inside. A DataFrame has a schema, so the Catalyst optimizer can push filters down, prune columns and generate code. Prefer DataFrames; use RDDs only for logic they can't express. Collect pulls every row to the driver, one machine, which runs out of memory and undoes the distributing. To look, use show or take. To save, write from the executors in parallel. Predicate pushdown filters at the file scan: Parquet, a columnar file format, stores each row group's minimum and maximum, so the reader skips groups that can't match. Projection pushdown reads only the columns the query names. DataFrames get both for free. Every answer here assumed the data had finished arriving. In streaming, it never does. Next: streaming and messaging.",
